## Bug

The public `LogEmissionTensor` preflight detected Boolean and complex values only when they appeared directly in an object array. MATLAB/HDF5-style inputs can instead store scalars inside zero-dimensional NumPy arrays. Those wrappers were not themselves instances of `bool` or `complex`, so they bypassed the guards.

Later floating-point coercion could therefore silently reinterpret nested Booleans as `0.0`/`1.0` and discard the imaginary component of nested complex likelihood or timing data. Other complex-valued tensor fields could reach later validators only after coercion had already been attempted, producing inconsistent errors or warnings.

## Fix

- unwrap nested zero-dimensional NumPy arrays before scalar-kind validation;
- retain cycle protection while unwrapping malformed object structures;
- apply the existing Boolean policy to likelihood and timing fields;
- apply the existing real-valued policy to all eight numeric tensor fields;
- preserve valid nested real NumPy scalars.

## Regression coverage

The new public-constructor tests verify that:

- nested complex values are rejected for `log_likelihood`, `spike_counts`, `times`, `dt`, `cell_ids`, `n_spikes`, `bin_durations`, and `transition_durations`;
- nested Boolean values are rejected for every field governed by the existing Boolean preflight;
- nested real NumPy scalar wrappers remain accepted and canonicalized normally.

## Validation

- pre-fix reproducer confirmed the complex detector returned false before `np.asarray(..., dtype=float)` discarded the imaginary component with `ComplexWarning`;
- pre-fix reproducer confirmed nested Booleans became `0.0` and `1.0`;
- `python -m py_compile` passed for the changed source and regression test;
- focused local validation passed for all rejection and valid-input cases;
- committed source and test blob SHAs match the locally validated files byte-for-byte;
- branch is 2 commits ahead and 0 behind current `main`, limited to the validator and focused test.

